 r←{dry}BackfillReadmes registryPath;ids;id;path;status;pause;due
⍝ Fetches READMEs for every major version line nobody has looked at yet, in one go.
⍝ Housekeeping does the same thing one package at a time (see `FetchPendingReadme`); this is
⍝ for an admin who would rather not wait for that, typically right after the feature arrives
⍝ on a Registry that already holds hundreds of packages.
⍝ A package that is not due is left alone, so this can be interrupted and started again
⍝ without fetching anything twice. Due means: never tried, or tried and failed and the
⍝ next of the three attempts has come round; see `ReadmeIsDue`. To force one regardless,
⍝ delete its "apl-readme.json" and run this: deleting that record is enough *here*, because
⍝ this function asks every package directly. It is not enough for the walk housekeeping
⍝ does, which is gated by `ReadmesAreDue` and will not notice until it next walks anyway.
⍝ Pass a 1 as ⍺ for a dry run: nothing is fetched and nothing is written.
⍝ Returns a two-column matrix: [;1] package ID, [;2] one of 'ok', 'none', 'error', 'skipped'
⍝ or 'would fetch'. Tally it with {≢⍵}⌸r[;2]. The 'none' rows are the ones worth reading:
⍝ a cluster of them sharing a group usually means a tagging convention of its own.
⍝ 'error' rows mean GitHub could not be reached; those come round again by themselves.
 dry←{0<⎕NC ⍵:⍎⍵ ⋄ 0}'dry'
 pause←1                                            ⍝ Seconds between fetches: a courtesy, not a requirement
 r←0 2⍴''
 :For id :In ids←GetNewestPerMajor registryPath
     path←registryPath,'/',id
     due←ReadmeIsDue path
     :If due∧dry
         status←'would fetch'
     :ElseIf due
         status←FetchAndStoreReadme path
         ⎕DL pause
     :Else
         status←'skipped'
     :EndIf
     r⍪←id status
 :EndFor
